As a Premier League team, [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] entered the tournament in the third round, beginning their FA Cup campaign with a dominant 5–1 away victory over Championship side [[Charlton Athletic F.C.|Charlton Athletic]], in what was [[Liam Rosenior]]'s first match in charge of the club. Goals came from [[Tosin Adarabioyo]], [[Marc Guiu]], [[Pedro Neto]], [[Enzo Fernández]], and [[Jorrel Hato]], the latter scoring his first for Chelsea.<ref>{{cite web |title=Chelsea begin Rosenior era with FA Cup rout of Charlton |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/report/_/gameId/762514 |work=ESPN|date=10 January 2026 |access-date=26 April 2026}}</ref> Pedro Neto notched his first Chelsea hat-trick in the fourth round away to [[Hull City F.C.|Hull City]]. [[Estêvão Willian|Estêvão]] also contributed to the scoring, the match finishing 4–0 to the Blues.<ref>{{cite web |title=Pedro Neto hat trick sees Chelsea cruise to win at Hull in FA Cup |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/report/_/gameId/401850946 |work=ESPN|date=13 February 2026 |access-date=26 April 2026}}</ref> In the fifth round, Chelsea were drawn away yet again, this time against Championship club [[Wrexham A.F.C.|Wrexham]], who proved to be much tougher opposition than those in previous rounds. [[Sam Smith (footballer, born 1998)|Sam Smith]] and [[Callum Doyle]] got their names on the scoresheet for the Welsh club, with a late [[Josh Acheampong]] goal forcing extra time. [[Alejandro Garnacho]] and [[João Pedro (footballer, born 2001)|João Pedro]] both scored in extra time to secure a 4–2 win for Chelsea.<ref>{{cite web |title=Chelsea end Wrexham's FA Cup run in extra time |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/report/_/gameId/401861589 |work=ESPN|date=7 March 2026 |access-date=26 April 2026}}</ref>

In the quarter-finals, Chelsea were drawn against tournament underdogs [[Port Vale F.C.|Port Vale]]. Chelsea made light work of the League One side, defeating them 7–0 at [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]], their largest FA Cup victory since [[2010–11 FA Cup|2011]], where Chelsea eased past [[Ipswich Town F.C.|Ipswich Town]] by the same scoreline.<ref>{{cite web |title=Chelsea 7-0 Ipswich (Jan 9, 2011) Final Score  |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/match/_/gameId/310125/ipswich-town-chelsea |work=ESPN |date=9 January 2011 |access-date=26 April 2026}}</ref> Jorrel Hato, João Pedro, Tosin Adarabioyo, [[Andrey Santos]], Estêvão, and Alejandro Garnacho all found the net during the match, along with an own goal from Port Vale defender [[Jordan Lawrence-Gabriel]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Chelsea hammer Port Vale 7-0 in FA Cup quarterfinal |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/report/_/gameId/401864076 |work=ESPN |date=4 April 2026 |access-date=26 April 2026}}</ref> Chelsea faced Premier League rivals [[Leeds United F.C.|Leeds United]] in the semi-finals at Wembley, where a lone goal from Enzo Fernández was enough to secure a 1–0 victory and send the Blues to the FA Cup final. Chelsea is seeking their first FA Cup victory since beating [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] 1–0 in [[2018 FA Cup final|2018]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Chelsea v Leeds United: FA Cup semi-final – live |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2026/apr/26/chelsea-v-leeds-fa-cup-semi-final-live |work=The Guardian |date=26 April 2026 |access-date=26 April 2026}}</ref>

