As a Premier League team, [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] entered the tournament in the third round. City began their FA Cup campaign with a rampant 10–1 display at home against League One side [[Exeter City F.C.|Exeter City]]. Manchester City's ten goals came from seven different players and two own goals, with [[Rico Lewis]] scoring two goals, and [[Jake Doyle-Hayes]] and [[Jack Fitzwater]] each scoring an own goal. [[Max Alleyne]], [[Rodri (footballer, born 1996)|Rodri]], [[Antoine Semenyo]], [[Tijjani Reijnders]], [[Nico O'Reilly]], and [[Ryan McAidoo]] all scored for the home team, with [[George Birch (footballer)|George Birch]] scoring a consolation for Exeter.<ref>{{cite web |title=Antoine Semenyo scores as Man City thrash Exeter 10-1 |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/report/_/gameId/762497 |work=ESPN |date=10 January 2026 |access-date=26 April 2026}}</ref> This was their biggest win since defeating [[Huddersfield Town A.F.C.|Huddersfield Town]] by the same score in 1987. City next faced [[Salford City F.C.|Salford City]] at home in the fourth round, and a goal from [[Marc Guéhi]] and an own goal from [[Alfie Dorrington]] helped overcome the League Two side to reach the fifth round, winning 2–0.<ref>{{cite web |title=City seal place in FA Cup fifth round with Salford victory |url=https://www.mancity.com/news/mens/manchester-city-202526-salford-city-match-report-63906672 |publisher=Manchester City |date=14 February 2026 |access-date=26 April 2026}}</ref> In the fifth round, City defeated fellow Premier League side [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] 3–1 at [[St James' Park]]. Despite an opener from Newcastle's [[Harvey Barnes]], City came back due to two goals by [[Omar Marmoush]], and a goal from [[Savinho]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Omar Marmoush brace sees City ease past Newcastle into FA Cup quarterfinals |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/report/_/gameId/401861586 |work=ESPN |date=7 March 2026 |access-date=26 April 2026}}</ref> 

In the quarter-finals, Manchester City were drawn against eight-time cup winners [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]], facing them at home. An [[Erling Haaland]] hat-trick and a goal from Antoine Semenyo helped City run out 4–0 victors, easing into their eighth FA Cup semi-final in a row.<ref>{{cite web |title=Haaland bags hat-trick as Man City hammer Liverpool 4-0 in FA Cup quarter-final |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/uk/haaland-bags-hat-trick-man-city-hammer-liverpool-4-0-fa-cup-quarter-final-2026-04-04/ |agency=Reuters |date=4 April 2026 |access-date=26 April 2026}}</ref> Despite a 79th minute goal from [[Finn Azaz]], City defeated Championship side [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]] 2–1 in the semi-finals at Wembley, as City mounted a late comeback through goals from [[Jérémy Doku]] and [[Nico González (footballer, born 2002)|Nico González]]. This marked the first time any team has reached four consecutive FA Cup finals, having previously played in the [[2023 FA Cup final|2023]], [[2024 FA Cup final|2024]] and [[2025 FA Cup final|2025]] finals respectively.<ref name="semi-final1-report"/>

==Match==
===Summary===
In the 72nd minute, Manchester City scored the only goal of the match when [[Antoine Semenyo]] back flicked the ball with his right leg into the left corner of the net after a low cross from the right from [[Erling Haaland]].<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/may/16/manchester-city-win-fa-cup-final-as-semenyo-magic-sinks-chelsea|title= Manchester City win FA Cup final as Semenyo’s moment of magic floors Chelsea|date=16 May 2026|work=Guardian|accessdate=22 May 2026}}</ref>
It was the only goal of the game as Manchester City won the game 1–0 and completed the domestic cup double after also winning the [[2026 EFL Cup final]] in March.<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.skysports.com/football/chelsea-vs-manchester-city/report/555725|title= Chelsea 0-1 Man City: Antoine Semenyo's brilliant flick seals FA Cup win and completes domestic cup double|date=16 May 2026|work=Sky Sports|accessdate=22 May 2026}}</ref>

